IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `written-law-interface`
IMPORT `cpc-types`
IMPORT `cpc-offence-procedure`
IMPORT `cpc-second-schedule`

-- Criminal Procedure Code 2010: an offence under ANOTHER written law, read with what that law says about its own offences.
-- Source: source/CPC2010.txt (the First Schedule, "Offences against laws other than the Penal Code 1871"; s 2(1) "arrestable offence", "bailable offence";
-- ss 7 to 9; the Second Schedule).
--
-- The question this module answers: WHAT IS THE PROCEDURAL TREATMENT OF AN OFFENCE UNDER AN ACT THAT HAS SAID HOW IT TREATS ITS OWN OFFENCES?  The last four
-- rows of the First Schedule give the treatment of an offence under another written law by the severity of its punishment, and leave room for the other law:
-- "unless specifically empowered ... by the law offended against" (the third and fourth bands); "or under any other written law" (s 2(1)); "shown to be triable ...
-- under that law" (ss 9(2)(b), 9(3)(b)).  cpc-offence-procedure.l4 takes those three answers as inputs.  Here they are SUPPLIED by the Act, as a
-- `Written Law Procedure Statement` (declared in written-law-interface.l4, carried identically in this row and in the rows of the Acts that make the statement:
-- sg/prevention-of-corruption-act-1960 and sg/misuse-of-drugs-act-1973).  The statement is a record of what the Act itself says, computed in the Act's own row from the
-- Act's own sections (PCA ss 32(1), 34; MDA ss 25(1), 53); it is not recomputed here.  What the Act does NOT say is read by the Code from the punishment.
--
-- Two Acts have made their statements.  Every other Act that creates offences needs one before this module can read it.

§ `Completing the jurisdiction facts from the Act's statement`

-- Two of the twelve Jurisdiction Facts are what the Act says: that the Act shows the offence as triable by a Magistrate's Court (s 9(2)(b)) or by a District Court
-- (s 9(3)(b)) "under that law".  The rest (death, life, fine-only, the maximum term, the Penal Code flag, the Public Prosecutor's authorisation and application,
-- the accused's consent) are facts of the case and are kept.  The Act's statement can only add to what the case already shows, never remove it.
@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, ss 9(2)(b), 9(3)(b)"
GIVEN j IS A `Jurisdiction Facts`, s IS A `Written Law Procedure Statement`
GIVETH A `Jurisdiction Facts`
`the jurisdiction facts completed from the Act's statement` MEANS
    `Jurisdiction Facts` WITH
        `the offence is punishable with death` IS j's `the offence is punishable with death`
        `the offence is punishable with imprisonment for life` IS j's `the offence is punishable with imprisonment for life`
        `the offence is a fine-only offence` IS j's `the offence is a fine-only offence`
        `the maximum term of imprisonment provided by law, in years` IS j's `the maximum term of imprisonment provided by law, in years`
        `the offence is under the Penal Code 1871` IS j's `the offence is under the Penal Code 1871`
        `the First Schedule shows the offence as triable by a Magistrate's Court` IS j's `the First Schedule shows the offence as triable by a Magistrate's Court`
        `the First Schedule shows the offence as triable by a District Court` IS j's `the First Schedule shows the offence as triable by a District Court`
        `a law other than the Penal Code 1871 shows the offence as triable by a Magistrate's Court under that law` IS
            (   j's `a law other than the Penal Code 1871 shows the offence as triable by a Magistrate's Court under that law`
             OR s's `the Act shows the offence as triable by a Magistrate's Court under that Act`)
        `a law other than the Penal Code 1871 shows the offence as triable by a District Court under that law` IS
            (   j's `a law other than the Penal Code 1871 shows the offence as triable by a District Court under that law`
             OR s's `the Act shows the offence as triable by a District Court under that Act`)
        `the Public Prosecutor has authorised in writing a Magistrate's Court to try the offence in the particular case` IS j's `the Public Prosecutor has authorised in writing a Magistrate's Court to try the offence in the particular case`
        `the Public Prosecutor applies to the District Court to try the offence` IS j's `the Public Prosecutor applies to the District Court to try the offence`
        `the accused consents, or if more than one are charged together with the same offence, all consent` IS j's `the accused consents, or if more than one are charged together with the same offence, all consent`

§ `The procedural treatment of an offence under the Act`

-- s 2(1): an arrestable offence is one for which a police officer may ordinarily arrest without a warrant, "shown as such in the First Schedule or under any other
-- written law".  An Act that DEEMS its offences arrestable (the Prevention of Corruption Act 1960, s 32(1)) makes every offence under it arrestable whatever
-- its punishment; an Act that EMPOWERS the police to arrest without warrant (the Misuse of Drugs Act 1973, s 25(1)) engages the First Schedule's third and fourth
-- bands' "unless specifically empowered ... by the law offended against", which matters only for an offence of less than 3 years' imprisonment.
@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, s 2(1) 'arrestable offence'; First Schedule, 'Offences against laws other than the Penal Code 1871'"
GIVEN j IS A `Jurisdiction Facts`, s IS A `Written Law Procedure Statement`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence under the Act is an arrestable offence` IF
        s's `the Act deems the offence to be an arrestable offence for the purposes of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010`
    OR  `the offence under another written law is an arrestable offence` j (s's `the Act empowers the police to arrest without warrant for the offence`)

@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, ss 7 to 9"
GIVEN j IS A `Jurisdiction Facts`, s IS A `Written Law Procedure Statement`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a Magistrate's Court may try the offence under the Act` IF
    `a Magistrate's Court may try the offence` (`the jurisdiction facts completed from the Act's statement` j s)

@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, ss 7 to 9"
GIVEN j IS A `Jurisdiction Facts`, s IS A `Written Law Procedure Statement`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a District Court may try the offence under the Act` IF
    `a District Court may try the offence` (`the jurisdiction facts completed from the Act's statement` j s)

@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, s 2(1) 'arrestable offence', 'bailable offence'; First Schedule, 'Offences against laws other than the Penal Code 1871'; ss 7 to 9"
GIVEN j IS A `Jurisdiction Facts`, s IS A `Written Law Procedure Statement`
GIVETH A `Procedural Treatment`
`the procedural treatment of an offence under the Act` MEANS
    `Procedural Treatment` WITH
        `the police may ordinarily arrest without warrant` IS `the offence under the Act is an arrestable offence` j s
        `a warrant ordinarily issues in the first instance` IS `a warrant ordinarily issues for the offence under another written law` j
        `the offence is bailable of right` IS `the offence under another written law is bailable` j
        `a Magistrate's Court may try the offence` IS `a Magistrate's Court may try the offence under the Act` j s
        `a District Court may try the offence` IS `a District Court may try the offence under the Act` j s
        `the General Division of the High Court may try the offence` IS TRUE

§ `The Second Schedule, for an Act that has made its statement`

-- The statement names the Act; the Second Schedule is read by Act.  The section is the section of the Act that creates the offence, without subsections.
@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, Second Schedule; ss 159, 211A"
GIVEN s IS A `Written Law Procedure Statement`, section IS A STRING
GIVETH A `Written Law Offence Facts`
`the written law offence facts for the Act's offence` MEANS
    `Written Law Offence Facts` WITH
        `the Act under which the offence is created` IS s's `the Act`
        `the section of that Act, without subsections` IS section
        `the offence is under the Act as in force immediately before the date the Second Schedule item names` IS FALSE

@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, Second Schedule, items 12 and 16A; ss 159(1), 211A(1)(b)"
GIVEN s IS A `Written Law Procedure Statement`, section IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act's offence is under a law specified in the Second Schedule` IF
    `the offence is under a law specified in the Second Schedule` (`the written law offence facts for the Act's offence` s section)
